/* ── The dashboard parts: what the package leaves to a register [kpadopt2-repairs] ──
   Formal keeps these parts exactly as Kenny approved them; this theme takes the three repairs the review asks for: a
   loading heat grid or matrix row as tall as its ready row (no skeleton gap between cells), an empty or failed run
   timeline with a lane-sized space for its sentence (never over the legend), the sentence as wide as its words on the
   theme's ground (never through a rule or a ghost bar), and an axis label near the end edge standing before its rule. */
@layer kp.signature {

/* ── The six opt-in options of the dashboard adoption, round 3, in this theme [kpadopt3] ──
   The nav bar's separator, the timeline's day buttons and series runs, the week agenda's upcoming list on a phone and
   the facet swatch's identity pattern (the choice-filter value and the heat grid's End are behaviour only).
   Blueprint's own (themes/blueprint/CHARACTER.md): the separator is the sheet's fold line, the dashed
   rule of the divider, stood upright and lying across the phone's list. A day on the axis is drafted: pointing brings
   the pen's cyan witness lines to either side of it; pressed it is the section, hatched in amber between the amber
   witness lines with its dimension line under it (the next run's amber). A series' finished run is a hatched section
   in its ink; the upcoming list's day rule is dashed; the facet pattern is framed by a drawn line. */
@layer kp.signature {

/* ── The fourth adoption round in this theme [kpadopt4]: data pieces ──
   Blueprint's own (themes/blueprint/CHARACTER.md): a part that does not exist yet, drawn on a ruled sheet and measured.
   A plate has no fill and no closed frame, the sheet shows through and a ruler runs along its start edge and its foot
   (G7); a section is hatched (G16); pointing sets the amber witness lines at a part's two ends (G8); a press takes the
   dimension (G14); a change is revised (G9); a warning is flagged with the triangle before its words, outlined for a
   warning, filled for a failure (G13); the lettering names, the sans speaks (G15). Every stroke is on the plotter's
   feed (G1) and under no-preference (G18). */
